import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.b9eNrXW1.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"The facilitator","description":"The guarantees verify enforces before any money moves, and what settlement does on Canton.","frontmatter":{"description":"The guarantees verify enforces before any money moves, and what settlement does on Canton."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"facilitator.md","filePath":"facilitator.md"}'),i={name:"facilitator.md"};function r(s,e,d,c,h,l){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="the-facilitator" tabindex="-1">The facilitator <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-facilitator" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The facilitator&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This page zooms in on steps 5 and 6 of <a href="/canton-x402-sdk/pr-133/payment-flow.html">the payment flow</a>, the facilitator&#39;s half. Before any money moves, verify enforces a handful of guarantees — your payment can&#39;t be replayed, tampered with, or under-paid — and settle re-runs all of them before executing on Canton. The facilitator never moves a payer&#39;s funds on its own authority: the only write that moves them is <code>executeAndWaitForTransaction</code> of a <strong>payer-signed</strong> transaction.</p><h2 id="what-verify-guarantees" tabindex="-1">What verify guarantees <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-verify-guarantees" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What verify guarantees&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These run in order, with no chain write. The first seven are shared code — the same <code>@chainsafe/x402-core</code> verifier the SDK exposes — so a payer can pre-flight its payload against them, and they refuse with the Canton scheme spec&#39;s codes, saying in <code>extensions.detail</code> which of a code&#39;s causes it was. The last is the facilitator&#39;s alone, since only it holds the state involved:</p><ol><li><strong>Right scheme and network</strong> — the payload, the requirements, and the network this facilitator is bound to all agree (<code>unsupported_scheme</code> or <code>invalid_network</code> otherwise).</li><li><strong>The transaction decodes</strong> — <code>preparedTransaction</code> is one gzip member within the size caps, its node graph is well-formed, and it holds the one transfer. Its sender is the payer (the payload names none), and checks 3 to 6 report it. A payload missing its proof is <code>invalid_exact_canton_missing_proof</code>, and one that does not decode is <code>invalid_exact_canton_malformed_payload</code>.</li><li><strong>Still executable</strong> — the signed transfer&#39;s own <code>executeBefore</code> is far enough in the future to settle (<code>invalid_exact_canton_expired</code>). The quote itself no longer expires; the deadline that counts is inside what the payer signed.</li><li><strong>The keys belong to the payer</strong> — the payload carries none. The facilitator reads the sender party&#39;s signing keys from the ledger, by that party id, and it is the lookup that binds them to the payer, so a caller can&#39;t sign as a party it doesn&#39;t control (<code>invalid_exact_canton_signature_invalid</code> when none can be resolved).</li><li><strong>The signature is valid</strong> — the hash recomputed from the transaction itself must equal <code>preparedTxHash</code>, and the payer&#39;s key must have signed it (<code>invalid_exact_canton_signature_invalid</code> otherwise).</li><li><strong>The signed transfer actually pays you.</strong> This is what binds the payment to your terms: the transfer&#39;s receiver, instrument and amount are checked against the requirements, so a valid signature over a divergent transfer is rejected: <code>invalid_exact_canton_merchant_mismatch</code>, <code>invalid_exact_canton_instrument_id_mismatch</code>, <code>invalid_exact_canton_amount_mismatch</code> or <code>invalid_exact_canton_memo_mismatch</code>. The amount must be EQUAL, not merely sufficient — <code>exact</code> is the scheme&#39;s distinguishing property, and a payer who overpays has no refund path. The transfer&#39;s <code>x402.memo</code> must equal your <code>extra.memo</code> exactly — in both directions, so a transfer carrying a memo you did not quote is refused as well.</li><li><strong>The transfer went through a real factory</strong> — the contract the transfer choice was exercised on must be one the transaction itself discloses, exercised through the Token Standard <code>TransferFactory</code> interface, and carrying the instrument&#39;s admin among its signatories (<code>invalid_exact_canton_malformed_payload</code>, with a <code>factory_</code> detail naming which part failed). Without it a payer could exercise a contract of their own that merely exposes a choice of the same name, and the payment would reduce to &quot;something called transfer happened&quot;. The disclosure travels inside what the payer signed, so this check takes it on the payer&#39;s word; the synchronizer authenticates it against the contract id at submission, which is where a fabricated one fails.</li><li><strong>No replay, and you&#39;re an allowed destination</strong> — the payment has not settled before (the ledger is the real guard: a replayed transfer names input holdings that are already spent), and for an authenticated merchant <code>payTo</code> must be a party that key is allowed to receive at (your destination policy). A destination denial comes back as <code>invalid_exact_canton_merchant_not_registered</code> with a <code>destination_not_allowed</code> detail, on verify and on settle alike.</li></ol><h2 id="what-settlement-does" tabindex="-1">What settlement does <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-settlement-does" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What settlement does&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Funds are real Splice <strong>Amulet</strong> (Canton Coin), transferred through the Token Standard&#39;s <code>TransferFactory</code> in one atomic Canton transaction. Your party must hold a live <code>TransferPreapproval</code>: with one the transfer is direct, so nothing is escrowed or locked up. Without one the Token Standard would make it an offer that completes only once you accept it — which is not a payment the facilitator can stand behind, since the resource is already served while the funds sit where the payer can withdraw them. Such a payment is refused at verify, with <code>invalid_exact_canton_preapproval_missing</code>. What is recorded is the signed transfer&#39;s amount, not the quoted floor, so an overpayment is not understated.</p>',7)])])}const u=t(i,[["render",r]]);export{f as __pageData,u as default};
